      <node unique_id="1111" master_id="0" name="Patriotism" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="0" ts_lastsave="0">
        <rich_text>
[10:04:25 AM] Joe Brewer says: Einstein quote of the day "The idea of achieving security through national armament is, at the present state of military technique, a disastrous illusion. "
[10:05:36 AM] Mark Adams says: "MR. Gobachev - tear down this wall" - Ronald Wilson Reagan - 40th and greatest president of United States
[10:22:21 AM] Mark Adams says: The practical side of Reagan's statement is that without a strong military, diplomacy has no teeth.  The purpose of the military is to enforce the diplomatic efforts.  Simply relying on the good will of others, whether they are individuals or countries, only leaves one open for betrayal.
[10:27:22 AM] Joe Brewer says: That doesn't seem to be a very christian way of thinking
[10:28:17 AM] Joe Brewer says: I don't disagree that a strong military creates fear and that fear is a powerful manipulator but you can not manipulate people into peace
[10:35:19 AM] Mark Adams says: I didn't realize we were debating christianity...but since you bring it up, there is real and true evil to deal with.  The purpose of the strong  military is not to manipulate anything...it's to give strength and support to the decisions made around the table.  Evil by it's very nature cannot be trusted, and without the fallback of a strength, then we (as individuals or countries) are open to attack by that evil.
[10:39:31 AM] Joe Brewer says: we aren't necessarily debating christianity but religion as I see must be intertwined accurately with all of an individuals world views otherwise you are just picking and choosing
[10:41:28 AM] Joe Brewer says: I don't disagree with your statements about evil the major problem with it though is who defines evil....you can only see through your own shaded lenses of a particular instense and therefore carry bias into every situation as does the other party and they may both be doing good in the minds of there community and don't fully understand the issues the other has to contend with
[7:33:22 PM] Mark Adams says: Joe , that line of thinking is called moral equivalency, the end result of which is the thought that everyone should be allowed to do whatever they want, whenever they want, without regard for consequences - simple basic lawlessness.  I have pretty simplistic views of good and evil...An excellent example of my definition of evil on an individual scale is the story in the news a few weeks ago where a sex offender, naked, entered the home of the  family of a 17 year old girl.  The girl awoke with the man in her bed room.  She screamed.  Her dad attacked the man in defense of his daughter, and in the process the sex offender died.  Evil would be the sex offender, who for his own pleasure sought to do irreparable harm to the girl.  Conversely, good would be the dad who actually was involved in the killing; however, his actions were in the defense of his daughter.  On a cultural/community/country scale, an example of evil would be September 11 when 19  radical islamic fascists killed 3000 Americans who were going bout their daily lives as they had done many times before.  Do I think there are good, wholesome law abiding Muslims? Of course I do.  Are there evil Muslims, yes.  Are there Christians who are good, yes.  Are there those who call themselves Christians who are evil - yes.  I'm sad to say that I have my own struggles against a sinful human nature, but I can still understand that some of those struggles are against evil.  A good reference for good and evil are the teachings of Jesus himself - whether one is a Christian or not.  If one lives one's life according to those teachings, then evil is not in him.  Boiling it down to two commandments, Jesus said to love God and love everyone else - that's what good is.  I daresay, if everyone was concerned about living a good life on a personal scale, then we wouldn't be worried about evil on a cultural scale.
[8:02:51 PM] Joe Brewer says: I agree with your with your statements completely...I completely agree that both examples you gave are completely accurate forms of evil.  The example of the girl and sex offender is totally horrible and i think father should be completely entittled to shot that person and be applauded for doing so.   I have many ideas and opinions on morality and life in general but few beliefs, because I haven't experinced enough to have definate belifes about many things.  One of the few beliefs I have is that no one should harm another or be allowed to do things to harm another unless under extreme duress in the act of defending oneself or another.  Now the example of 9/11 again I believe that this was an evil act.  My point lies in the reaction to evil that propagates more evil and escalates wars and  distrust among men.  We were horribly attacked in 2001 and america came together and said that was unacceptable but do you really think invading two countries was the appropriate good response, do you think that is what Jesus would have recommended as the answer to that attack?  We took all that american good sentiment packed it into our globally superior army and destroyed to soverign nations and still haven't brought those who commited those attacks to justice.  When we bombed these soverign nations that harbored both honorable and unhonorable people we killed many of both.  Now there are thousands of families in these countries that had no connection to 9/11 what so ever that are missing fathers and sons, mothers and daughters because of our attacks on their nations. They are mad and look at the US as having done evil to them in our attempt at retribution of the 9/11 attacks on our country are they wrong?  We look at this same situation and think that we are doing good in that we have made war on the groups that were involved on 9/11 and have ousted corrupt leaders of these sovergn nations are we wrong?  This is where I speak of the understanding of other people and and the bias that all people carry in evaluating good and evil. This is one of my fundamental problems with major organized religion is that it seems to put people at direct opposite positions of each other even when most of them share the same "god" but chose to see him differently there again invoking their cultural bias.
[8:21:31 PM] Mark Adams says: Our response to the 9/11 attack was unquestionably the right thing to do.  By doing so, we have prevented further attacks on our soil and protected the lives of our families.  The US attack on Afganistan and Iraq was an effort to remove regimes which supported the efforts of Bin Laden and his ilk.  There have been numerous errors of judgement and strategy made by the administration, but the results are clear that the US has been protected from further attacks at home.  There will always be those who seek to do us harm, and one primary role of government is to insure the protection of it's people, through force, if necessary.   Do I beleive that America is infallible and all we do is good with perfect results - of course not.  However, I know that no other country spends more on humanitarian aid.  The citizen's of the US give more money per capita to charity that the citizens of any other country.  We are a blessed nation, and we have a responsibility to lead the world, but sometimes we have to defend ourselves, also.  On an individual scale, if my children were being bullied by someone, I would hope they would fight back - not to escalate the violence, but in the hopes that by doing so the other person may lose interest in having the fight, and put their energy in something more productive.


</rich_text>
      </node>
      <node unique_id="1110" master_id="0" name="Philisophical Questions" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="0" ts_lastsave="0">
        <rich_text>life could be a series of positive and negative energies

Life is a particle and a wave?

Life has destiny and we creat our own?

are there justifiable reasons for murder?

life is a constantly changing never ending puzzle
</rich_text>
      </node>
      <node unique_id="1106" master_id="0" name="Serendipity" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="0" ts_lastsave="0">
        <rich_text>
The role of serendipity in science and technology

One aspect of Walpole's original definition of serendipity that is often missed in modern discussions of the word is the "sagacity" of being able to link together apparently innocuous facts to come to a valuable conclusion. Thus, while some scientists and inventors are reluctant about reporting accidental discoveries, others openly admit its role; in fact serendipity is a major component of scientific discoveries and inventions. According to M.K. Stoskopf[3] "it should be recognized that serendipitous discoveries are of significant value in the advancement of science and often present the foundation for important intellectual leaps of understanding".

The amount of contribution of serendipitous discoveries varies extensively among the several scientific disciplines. Pharmacology and chemistry are probably the fields where serendipity is more common.

Most authors who have studied scientific serendipity both in a historical, as well as in an epistemological point of view, agree that a prepared and open mind is required on the part of the scientist or inventor to detect the importance of information revealed accidentally. This is the reason why most of the related accidental discoveries occur in the field of specialization of the scientist. About this, Albert Hofmann, the Swiss chemist who discovered LSD properties by unintentionally ingesting it at his lab, wrote:

"It is true that my discovery of LSD was a chance discovery, but it was the outcome of planned experiments and these experiments took place in the framework of systematic pharmaceutical, chemical research. It could better be described as serendipity."

The French scientist Louis Pasteur also famously said: "In the fields of observation chance favors only the prepared mind."[4] This is often rendered as "Chance favors the prepared mind."

History, of course, does not record accidental exposures of information which could have resulted in a new discovery, and we are justified in suspecting that they are many. There are several examples of this, however, and prejudice of preformed concepts are probably the largest obstacle. See for example [1] for a case where this happened (the rejection of an accidental discovery in the field of self-stimulation of the brain in humans).



</rich_text>
      </node>
