              <node unique_id="1219" master_id="0" name="Linguistics of Jesus" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="0" ts_lastsave="0">
                <rich_text>Linguistics of Jesus
Since the Gospels were written in Greek, they had to write his name in Greek. The Greek alphabet is not equipped to write the sounds of Hebrew, so modifications were necessary. For example, many Hebrew words begin with a Y sound... but Greek can't adequately represent that, so it had to use an I. (Sometimes the Greek G can be a Y, but not preceding an epsilon or eta, so they couldn't use gamma.) Second, the "sh" sound represented by the Hebrew letter shin is foreign to Greek. There was no way to represent that sound in writing... so they changed it to an s. Why s? Because the Hebrew letter shin has a counterpart, sin, which is an s, and looks the same as shin. So replacing the sh with s was logical. The next problem was the H in the middle of the Hebrew name. Modern Greek has no H sound, and ancient Greek only allowed it at the beginning of a word. It was represented with a small mark, but there is no letter in the alphabet to represent the H, so there was no way the Greek form of the name could have an H in the middle. In Hebrew, the name ends in a vowel sound. But masculine Greek nouns can't end in a vowel, so an s was added to the end. (Most masculine Greek nouns end in s, or os.) So Yehoshua became Iesous, because that was as close as Greek could get to representing the name in writing, and as close as Greek speaking people could get to pronouncing it without learning to speak a new language.

The Greek Iesous became the Latin Iesus. In the middle ages, the J was added to Latin, but had the sound of a Y. English and French retained the Latin spelling, but each pronounces the J in their own way. Italian replaced the J with G, pronounced like our J, and dropped the final s. Spanish kept the Latin spelling, but pronounces the J like an H.

Languages have to make adaptations to the name, because many languages don't have to sounds necessary to say the original, nor the symbols to represent those sounds in writing.

Even modern Hebrew no longer uses the original pronunciation, but uses Yeshua, to differentiate between Jesus and the prophet Joshua, since in the original, the names are identical. (But coincidentally, the modern Hebrew pronunciation also happens to be one of the Hebrew words for salvation.)

OK, language lesson over... you can wake up now! ;-)

A Review of Darwin's Black Box: The Biochemical Challenge To Evolution, by David W. Ussery, Ph.D. Associate Professor Microbial Genomics Group Leader. (The Free Press, New York, 1996), For Bios Magazine; most of the text for this review was written in July, 1998, and last UPDATED in 2000

Irreducible Complexity Demystified, by Pete Dunkelberg, 2003.





</rich_text>
              </node>
