                <node unique_id="592" master_id="0" name="ND - Napoleon" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="0" ts_lastsave="0">
                  <rich_text>ND-Napoleon
   Path: \Musing of the Meaning\Time\Known History\1500 AD\1700 AD\1731 AD\1769 AD\Napoleon (1769-1821)\)
Leaf 1:
Napoleon Bonaparte (1769 - 1821)

	This quotation is vintage Nostradamus. He is employing one of his favourite devices: the anagram. "PAU, NAY, LORON" when r	earranged becomes NAPAULON ROY, or Napoleon the king, given the Corsican spelling of his name, Napauleone. The text also 	describes him as a man of 'fire', or of war, rather 'than of the blood', or of royal lineage. The 'Piuses' of the third line are the Popes Pius 	VI and Pius VII, who were both imprisoned by Napoleon as the fourth line suggests.
http://members.tripod.com/~mhkerekes/napoleontl.htm




C8Q1
Pau, Nay, Loron will be more of fire than blood,
to swim in praise, the great one to flee to the confluence (of rivers).
He will refuse entry to the magpies
Pampon and the Durance will keep them confined.






</rich_text>
                </node>
