                <node unique_id="616" master_id="0" name="18140913 CE" prog_lang="custom-colors" tags="" readonly="0" nosearch_me="0" nosearch_ch="0" custom_icon_id="0" is_bold="0" foreground="" ts_creation="0" ts_lastsave="0">
                  <rich_text>





September 13, 1814:  Francis Scott Key pens a poem which is later set to music and in 1931 becomes America’s national anthem, “The Star-Spangled Banner.”  The poem, originally titled “The Defence of Fort McHenry,” was written after Key witnessed the Maryland fort being bombarded by the British during the War of 1812.  Key was inspired by the sight of a lone U.S. flag still flying over Fort McHenry at daybreak.












</rich_text>
